    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d639">

                <pb facs="0964" n="844" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg844"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.74/210: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-01-06T13:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 6,
                                1922—1 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received January 6—5:58
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>5. With reference to my telegram of December 5, no. 432,<anchor n="13"
                        xml:id="d639-f1"/> I am informed by a representative sent to me by the new
                    Minister of <pb facs="0965" n="845" xml:id="pg845"/> Communications that,
                    according to telegrams from the agents of the Ministry at the Conference on the
                    Limitation of Armament, the American Government has apparently indicated that it
                    approves the French proposals for the unification of the Chinese
                        wireless.<anchor n="14" xml:id="d639-f2"/> As the Minister of Communications
                    disapproves these proposals he is disturbed and desires to proceed with the
                    Federal Telegraph agreement. He urges the financial support of the American
                    Government in behalf of the Federal Company in order to permit the building of
                    the station at Shanghai, allowing the building of the other stations to wait
                    until later, in this way reducing to about $3,000,000 the initial financial
                    requirements.</p>
                <p>In the event that this unification scheme is to supersede the Federal contract,
                    it is deemed desirable for the information of the Chinese critics that the
                    Department should issue a statement explaining the change. On the other hand if
                    the Federal contract still holds the field, it is essential that work should be
                    started without delay, in order to maintain American prestige.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
